  "narrative": {
    "intro": "HUBZone set-aside obligations through FY2026 to date totaled $538M across 1,436 awards, with an average award size of $375K. Obligations fell 41.5% year over year. The Department of Defense accounted for the largest single share of HUBZone buying, obligating $173M, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $110M and the Department of Labor at $54.2M. Other large buyers included the Department of Transportation, Department of Health and Human Services, and Department of Commerce. On the vendor side, T&H SERVICES, LLC led recipients with $30.2M, with PANAMERICA COMPUTERS, INC. and ANACAPA MICRO PRODUCTS, INC. also among the top recipients. Other Computer Related Services was the largest industry by obligations at $161M, with construction, IT services, and facilities support among the next-largest industry groups. FY2026 to date figures are partial and reflect fiscal year to date obligations rather than a full-year total.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in HUBZone obligations in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 41.5% year over year. Total HUBZone obligations totaled $538M in FY2026 to date, down from prior full-year totals like $920M in FY25 and $1.1B in FY23. Award activity produced 1,436 awards at an average award size of $375K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers, vendors, and industries in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $173M, the Department of Homeland Security obligated $110M, and the Department of Labor obligated $54.2M. T&H SERVICES, LLC received $30.2M, PANAMERICA COMPUTERS, INC. received $24.7M, and ANACAPA MICRO PRODUCTS, INC. received $21.1M. Other Computer Related Services led industries with $161M, followed by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $57.9M and Computer Systems Design Services at $48.3M."
      }
    ]
  },
